Title 
Optimizing a Student Course Management System Using Views and Indexing for 
Faster Query Performance 


Aim 
To understand and implement views and indexing in a Student Course Management 
System to optimize query performance and enable efficient data retrieval. 


Objectives 
1. To understand the concept of views in SQL and their practical use for reporting. 
2. To create, update, and delete records using views wherever applicable. 
3. To understand the concept of indexing and its impact on query performance. 
4. To implement indexing on frequently searched columns in the database. 
5. To measure query performance improvements with indexing and views. 


Problem Statement 
A Student Course Management System contains multiple tables such as Students, 
Courses, and Enrollments. Retrieving detailed student and course information often 
requires complex joins, which can slow down queries. 
The task is to: 
● Create views to simplify reporting on student enrollment and course details. 
● Update and delete records through views where possible. 
● Apply indexing on frequently queried columns (e.g., student_id, course_id) to 
optimize search and query performance. 
● Measure query execution time before and after indexing to demonstrate 
performance improvement. 
Prerequisites 
● Basic knowledge of SQL commands (DDL, DML) 


Theory Concepts 
1. Views 
● A view is a virtual table based on the result of a SQL query. 
● Views do not store data physically but provide a way to simplify complex queries. 
● Benefits of Views: 
o Simplifies queries for reporting 
o Provides security by restricting column visibility 
o Can be used for updating and deleting records (updatable views) 
Example Syntax: 
CREATE VIEW StudentCourseView AS 
SELECT s.student_id, s.name, c.course_id, c.course_name 
FROM Students s 
JOIN Enrollments e ON s.student_id = e.student_id 
JOIN Courses c ON e.course_id = c.course_id; 
2. Indexing 
● An index is a database object that improves the speed of data retrieval. 
● Indexes are created on columns that are frequently searched, joined, or used in 
WHERE clauses. 
● Types of Indexes: 
o Single-column index – created on one column 
o Composite index – created on multiple columns 
o Unique index – ensures unique values for a column 
Example Syntax: 
CREATE INDEX idx_student_id ON Students(student_id); 
CREATE INDEX idx_course_id ON Courses(course_id); 
Benefits of Indexing: 
● Reduces the amount of data scanned during queries 
● Improves SELECT query performance 
● Can slightly increase storage and update overhead 


Methodology / Procedure 
1. Create Views: 
o Create views to join Students, Courses, and Enrollments for easy 
reporting. 
2. Update/Delete Records Using Views: 
o Update student or course information through updatable views where 
allowed. 
3. Create Indexes: 
o Identify frequently searched columns (student_id, course_id) 
o Create single-column or composite indexes 
4. Query Testing and Performance Measurement: 
o Execute queries before indexing and record execution time 
o Execute queries after indexing and record improved execution time 
5. Analyze Results: 
o Compare execution times to quantify performance gains 
Result 
● Views were successfully created for simplified reporting of students and courses. 
● Records could be updated and deleted through updatable views. 
● Indexes significantly improved query performance for searches on student_id 
and course_id. 
● Execution plans showed reduced scan time after indexing, demonstrating 
optimization. 


Conclusion 
Views and indexing are powerful techniques to optimize database performance: 
● Views simplify complex queries, improve readability, and provide a secure 
interface for users. 
● Indexing drastically reduces query execution time for frequently accessed 
columns. 
● Combining views and indexes improves both the usability and performance of 
a Student Course Management System. 
